{
  "meta": {
    "description": "Graphify vs no-Graphify token cost. For each question the graph answers in one compact query_graph call; the 'no graph' baseline is the source files that query's answer nodes come from - i.e. the code an agent would have to read to answer the same question. That baseline is generous to the no-graph side: it assumes the agent already knows exactly which files to open, which is itself what the graph provides. Regenerate query set as needed; ratios depend on the ORE checkout (ORE_ENGINE). s01-s08 are the original suite (kept unchanged for history); s09-s50 widen coverage to every merged chunk and vary mode/depth/phrasing, added to catch term-weight drift outside the trade-pricing core the original 8 focus on (see CLAUDE.md rule 3). A question also carries a rubric, since token cost alone cannot tell a better answer from a cheaper wrong one. `required_nodes` is what the answer must contain and gates the command; `xfail_nodes` is what it should contain but does not yet, which is reported and never gates, so a half-right question still protects its working half. Entries are `label`, `label@source-file` (labels match whole, so `Swap` is not satisfied by `SwapIndex`), or `src:<path fragment>` for questions about a body of code rather than a symbol. Both lists state the answer a domain reader would expect; only the split between them reflects what the graph does today, so moving an entry from `xfail_nodes` to `required_nodes` is how an improvement gets locked in. A rubric naming a `fieldmap/...` path is skipped when the graph was merged without a fieldmap snapshot.",
    "version": 5
  },
